WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.660
Right now, as you're listening to this, you probably have a browser window open somewhere with, I don't know, 20, maybe 30 tabs just sitting there.

00:08.740 --> 00:09.780
Oh, at least 30.

00:09.940 --> 00:19.860
Right, at least. And I'm willing to bet that every time you look at that crowded little bar at the top of your screen, you feel this tiny, nagging sense of guilt.

00:19.980 --> 00:21.640
Yeah, that classic tab anxiety.

00:21.840 --> 00:27.780
Exactly. You feel like you're procrastinating or you're disorganized or just, you know, failing to process information properly.

00:27.780 --> 00:32.600
But what if I told you that those open tabs are actually not a bad habit at all?

00:32.800 --> 00:34.380
I mean, that sounds too good to be true.

00:34.820 --> 00:44.600
Well, what if I told you that having 80 tabs open is, it's actually a mathematically predictable symptom of how human consciousness physically operates?

00:44.880 --> 00:47.160
It's a very validating thought, isn't it?

00:47.160 --> 00:54.800
To realize that your messy digital life is actually like a perfect reflection of this deep topological struggle happening inside your brain.

00:54.800 --> 00:58.220
It is intensely validating, which is why we're diving straight into it today.

00:58.520 --> 00:59.540
Welcome to the Deep Dive.

00:59.840 --> 01:06.360
Our mission today is to, well, basically completely rewire how you understand your own mind.

01:06.620 --> 01:09.780
Yeah, your mind, your memory, the tools you use every day.

01:09.900 --> 01:15.160
Right. Even artificial intelligence and the very fabric of human civilization, honestly.

01:15.160 --> 01:27.680
Absolutely. We're looking at a 2026 theoretical monograph titled, Everything is a Clipboard, Recursive State, Functional Essays, and the Computational Geometry of Externalized Cognition.

01:27.880 --> 01:29.160
Which is quite the title.

01:29.280 --> 01:33.980
It's a massive mouthful. And the authors of this independent research are going by the name Fliction.

01:34.520 --> 01:39.120
And look, I know, everything is a clipboard. Sounds completely absurd at first glance.

01:39.220 --> 01:42.500
It really does. It sounds like a tech brow taking a metaphor way too far.

01:42.500 --> 01:51.640
Exactly. But by the time we are done today, I promise you, you will never look at your memory or, you know, a written essay the same way ever again.

01:51.780 --> 01:55.040
Because Fliction is attempting something incredibly ambitious here.

01:55.260 --> 02:04.420
They're trying to unify theoretical computer science, cognitive psychology, and dynamical systems math into this single architecture.

02:04.640 --> 02:07.160
Right. A unified theory of basically everything cognitive.

02:07.160 --> 02:13.040
Yeah. And they start by tearing down the absolute most fundamental assumption we have about ourselves, which is how we remember things.

02:13.220 --> 02:16.360
Okay. Let's unpack this because we need to talk about the filing cabinet.

02:16.440 --> 02:18.660
Oh, the classic filing cabinet metaphor.

02:18.700 --> 02:21.740
Whenever we talk about human memory, we use the language of a warehouse.

02:22.300 --> 02:30.940
You experience something, you pack it into a little mental cardboard box, slap a label on it, and shove it into the dark archives of your brain.

02:30.940 --> 02:36.360
Right. And then when you need it, you just walk back into the archives, open the drawer, and pull the complete file right back out.

02:36.580 --> 02:39.000
We talk about our minds like they're hard drives.

02:39.200 --> 02:41.700
Literally. Let me retrieve that file.

02:42.920 --> 02:49.800
But Fliction opens this monograph with just a scorched earth attack on that entire idea.

02:50.020 --> 02:52.260
Because the storage metaphor is completely wrong.

02:52.460 --> 02:57.740
And the only reason we cling to it is simply because it maps onto the physical artifacts we use in our daily lives.

02:58.080 --> 03:00.360
We put paper in a folder so we think our brain does it.

03:00.360 --> 03:08.300
Exactly. We save a file to a hard drive, we load it. It's a binary state. The thing is either in the storage medium or it's lost forever.

03:09.020 --> 03:13.780
But when cognitive scientists actually study the brain, that isn't what happens at all.

03:13.860 --> 03:15.080
No. Not even close.

03:15.500 --> 03:25.660
Fliction argues against both the classical view of artificial intelligence, you know, the one that treats the mind as a machine just shuffling discrete symbols around in memory slots.

03:25.740 --> 03:25.960
Right.

03:25.960 --> 03:33.940
And they also argue against the more modern neural network view, which says cognition is just like activation running through statistical patterns.

03:34.260 --> 03:39.740
Because Fliction says both of those completely miss this hidden primitive of intelligence.

03:40.680 --> 03:42.780
Which brings us to thesis two of the paper.

03:43.800 --> 03:45.560
The mind does not store the world.

03:46.120 --> 03:47.260
Memory is not a photograph.

03:47.260 --> 03:50.500
So if we aren't storing things, what on earth are we doing?

03:50.560 --> 03:51.760
We are reconstructing.

03:51.860 --> 03:55.840
The core mechanism of memory, according to this framework, is a traversal compression.

03:56.040 --> 03:58.140
Okay. Break that down for me. Traversal compression.

03:58.440 --> 04:05.880
Well, let's think about like an expert mathematician who encounters a wildly complex theorem they originally solved five years ago.

04:06.080 --> 04:11.200
They are not retrieving a high-resolution JPEG of the solution from their temporal lobe.

04:11.200 --> 04:12.880
They aren't downloading the answer.

04:12.880 --> 04:17.120
Right. Instead, they're re-entering a previously stabilized pathway.

04:17.380 --> 04:21.920
They're literally reconstructing the logic in real time, but they're guided by constraints.

04:22.400 --> 04:26.800
I was trying to visualize this while reading the paper because the filing cabinet just completely fails here.

04:26.960 --> 04:28.380
So let me try this analogy on you.

04:28.460 --> 04:29.240
Okay. Let's hear it.

04:29.900 --> 04:35.220
Imagine your mind is this vast, incredibly dense, completely uncharted forest.

04:35.220 --> 04:41.800
Memory isn't a photograph of that forest. Memory is a series of blazes.

04:42.020 --> 04:44.120
Blazes, like the marks on trees.

04:44.360 --> 04:48.060
Exactly. Those little axe marks cut into the bark of the trees on a hiking trail.

04:48.780 --> 04:55.460
The blazes don't store the forest. The forest is still wildly complicated and overgrown.

04:55.800 --> 05:04.000
But those marks allow you to re-navigate a very specific winding path through that confusing terrain without getting lost.

05:04.000 --> 05:05.120
Oh, I like that.

05:05.640 --> 05:08.980
The blazes act as the constraint. They don't do the walking for you.

05:09.060 --> 05:11.900
I mean, you still have to expend the actual cognitive energy to hike the trail.

05:12.020 --> 05:12.840
Right. You're still walking.

05:13.060 --> 05:14.940
But they stop you from wandering off a cliff.

05:15.120 --> 05:18.680
And this leads us directly to thesis one of the whole paper, the clipboard primitive.

05:18.900 --> 05:19.680
And we should clarify.

05:20.320 --> 05:24.580
In Fliction's terminology, a clipboard is not the copy-paste function on your Mac.

05:24.580 --> 05:33.000
No, not at all. A clipboard is defined as a persistent, re-enterable partial state that functions as a callable computational operator.

05:33.380 --> 05:35.300
It's a synchronization surface.

05:35.680 --> 05:40.220
Synchronization surface, meaning it's the thing I sync up with to start the hike again.

05:40.220 --> 05:49.780
Precisely. It preserves just enough of those blazes on the trees so your current mind is forced to reconstruct the thought without having to figure out the whole forest from scratch.

05:49.780 --> 05:55.520
And this isn't just like some wild mathematical theory, right? Flixen ties this back to hard psychology.

05:55.740 --> 06:09.140
Oh, yeah. It aligns flawlessly with decades of experimental psychology. Flixen spends time validating this against Daniel Schachter's work on constructive memory and Indel Tolving's concept of ephoric synchrony.

06:09.140 --> 06:13.940
Ephoric synchrony. Let's unpack that one because it's a beautiful concept, even if the name is a little dense.

06:14.360 --> 06:20.140
Yeah. So ephoric is the process by which a retrieval cue interacts with a memory trace to produce a conscious memory.

06:20.640 --> 06:27.180
Tolving proved that the memory doesn't actually exist fully formed until the present cue and the past trace synchronize.

06:27.280 --> 06:28.600
So the memory is built in the moment.

06:29.040 --> 06:31.620
Exactly. Frederick Bartlett showed this a century ago.

06:31.620 --> 06:35.680
When people recount a story, they don't play it back like a tape recorder.

06:35.880 --> 06:40.680
They actively rebuild it, frequently changing details to fit their current expectations.

06:40.920 --> 06:44.260
Right. Which is why eyewitness testimony is notoriously terrible.

06:44.480 --> 06:50.560
Exactly. So the clipboard, Infliction's Math, is just the geometric shape of that constraint.

06:50.740 --> 06:53.000
It's the blueprint that guides the reconstruction.

06:53.000 --> 07:02.460
To bring this right down to the listener's daily life, think about what actually happens when you reread an old journal entry from, say, 10 years ago.

07:02.940 --> 07:05.900
You aren't downloading static data into your brain.

07:06.040 --> 07:06.920
No, not at all.

07:06.980 --> 07:12.740
As your eyes move over your own words, you are literally reentering your own past cognitive trajectory.

07:13.020 --> 07:16.180
You feel the ghost of the thought process happening all over again.

07:16.400 --> 07:17.820
You are activating a clipboard?

07:17.820 --> 07:25.880
Right. The ink on the page is just the compressed metadata telling your current mind how to walk the exact same inferential steps you took a decade ago.

07:26.380 --> 07:28.380
Which forces us to ask a really weird question.

07:28.980 --> 07:33.160
If thinking is walking a path, what exactly is the ground we are walking on?

07:33.320 --> 07:35.680
Like, what is the terrain of the forest made of?

07:35.960 --> 07:38.460
And Fliction calls it the ambiguity space.

07:38.660 --> 07:40.600
Yes. The ambiguity space.

07:40.820 --> 07:43.280
Now, in everyday language, ambiguity is a bad thing, right?

07:43.680 --> 07:45.980
If your boss gives you ambiguous instructions, you're annoyed.

07:45.980 --> 07:52.000
Classical logic treats ambiguity as a defect, an error that needs to be eliminated.

07:52.620 --> 07:54.380
And that is the radical departure here.

07:54.820 --> 07:57.420
In this framework, ambiguity is not a defect.

07:57.980 --> 08:00.880
It is the fundamental topological medium of cognition.

08:01.180 --> 08:03.380
It's the literal space we think inside of.

08:03.520 --> 08:06.760
Right. It is the space of all possible semantic states.

08:06.960 --> 08:10.820
It's the raw clay of meaning before you've sculpted it into anything.

08:10.960 --> 08:13.720
But you can't just wander endlessly through that raw clay, right?

08:13.760 --> 08:15.180
You'd just be spouting gibberish.

08:15.180 --> 08:20.940
Right. Which is why navigating the ambiguity space is governed by something Fliction calls admissibility.

08:21.420 --> 08:21.900
Admissibility.

08:22.080 --> 08:26.600
Yeah. Admissibility is the constraint on how you can transition from one thought to the next.

08:27.000 --> 08:30.620
But critically, it is not a rigid, binary, true or false.

08:30.780 --> 08:35.180
It is a graded, context-relative measure of inferential coherence.

08:35.520 --> 08:38.440
Graded coherence. So, not a simple one or zero.

08:38.620 --> 08:41.040
Give me an example of how that gradient works in our heads.

08:41.040 --> 08:44.380
Think about the difference between strict logic and poetry.

08:44.860 --> 08:51.160
If I say, all dogs are mammals, a golden retriever is a dog, therefore a golden retriever is a mammal,

08:52.000 --> 08:55.840
that transition has a maximally high admissibility score.

08:55.980 --> 08:58.000
It's just a rock-solid logical path.

08:58.080 --> 09:00.700
Exactly. It preserves structural coherence perfectly.

09:00.700 --> 09:02.220
You can walk that path easily.

09:02.780 --> 09:05.680
But what if I use Shakespeare and I say, Juliet is the sun?

09:06.220 --> 09:08.480
Well, literally speaking, that's false.

09:08.980 --> 09:12.060
She is not a giant ball of incandescent plasma.

09:12.280 --> 09:14.700
So, in a classical computer, that would crash the system.

09:14.820 --> 09:15.580
It would just throw an error.

09:15.900 --> 09:18.020
Exactly. Error, incompatible types.

09:18.280 --> 09:20.840
But in the human mind, it is partially admissible.

09:20.980 --> 09:26.060
It violates literal truth, sure, but it preserves emotional and thematic coherence.

09:26.060 --> 09:29.080
So, it's a valid path through the forest, just a slightly wilder one.

09:29.140 --> 09:34.220
Right. But if I said, Juliet is a stapler, that has near zero admissibility.

09:34.480 --> 09:37.020
The semantic coherence completely collapses.

09:37.140 --> 09:38.580
You stop walking and say, wait, what?

09:38.920 --> 09:41.560
Okay, so we are trying to move through this space,

09:41.940 --> 09:46.140
sticking to paths that have high admissibility so we don't start thinking nonsense.

09:46.660 --> 09:49.240
But Flixian says the terrain itself is warped.

09:49.240 --> 09:53.820
They use these terms, semantic curvature and pressure points.

09:54.460 --> 09:56.580
What is semantic curvature?

09:57.340 --> 10:00.440
Well, some regions of the ambiguity space are completely flat,

10:00.700 --> 10:03.420
the concepts are distinct, the transitions are obvious,

10:03.420 --> 10:07.080
and it takes almost zero cognitive energy to move through them.

10:07.240 --> 10:08.620
Like thinking of what you want for breakfast.

10:08.840 --> 10:10.640
Exactly. Very low semantic curvature.

10:11.340 --> 10:13.220
But other regions are highly curved.

10:13.220 --> 10:18.020
In a high curvature region, a tiny microscopic shift in your framing

10:18.020 --> 10:21.020
leads to wildly diverging conclusions.

10:21.720 --> 10:24.000
Flixian calls these regions pressure points.

10:24.200 --> 10:25.720
Wait, I really want to latch onto this.

10:25.780 --> 10:28.960
Are you saying that when I sit down to learn something incredibly complex,

10:29.240 --> 10:31.360
like, see, I'm trying to understand quantum mechanics,

10:31.520 --> 10:33.780
or a really dense philosophical argument,

10:34.180 --> 10:36.040
and my brain physically hurts?

10:36.180 --> 10:37.180
Yes, the brain strain.

10:37.380 --> 10:40.200
Right. Is it because I'm navigating a space with high curvature,

10:40.200 --> 10:43.760
where it's incredibly easy to just fall off the logical path?

10:44.080 --> 10:45.080
Yes, absolutely.

10:45.360 --> 10:49.820
High curvature means the local geometry of the concept is intrinsically unstable.

10:50.240 --> 10:53.120
You're being forced to make rapid, minute decisions

10:53.120 --> 10:55.840
that have massive inferential consequences.

10:56.660 --> 11:00.720
It requires intense energetic focus to stay on an admissible path.

11:01.120 --> 11:01.700
Exactly.

11:02.360 --> 11:07.140
Flixian formally defines a traversal as an admissibility-preserving path

11:07.140 --> 11:08.180
through this space.

11:08.180 --> 11:13.160
You are literally trying to balance on a tightrope over a canyon of nonsense.

11:13.340 --> 11:14.480
That's exactly what it is.

11:14.580 --> 11:16.060
And what happens when you finally get it?

11:16.280 --> 11:19.580
You know that feeling when a difficult concept finally just clicks?

11:19.900 --> 11:21.740
That is the phenomenology of insight.

11:22.100 --> 11:25.520
We usually describe insight as this magical spark, right?

11:25.600 --> 11:26.680
A light bulb going off.

11:26.740 --> 11:27.840
Right, a eureka moment.

11:28.060 --> 11:30.120
But Flixian strips the magic away and says,

11:30.120 --> 11:34.280
Mathematically, insight is simply the discovery of a stable traversal

11:34.280 --> 11:36.340
through a previously unstable region.

11:36.960 --> 11:39.180
You found a path that doesn't violate coherence.

11:39.560 --> 11:43.020
And once you find it, your brain immediately tries to stabilize it

11:43.020 --> 11:45.380
so you don't have to balance on that tightrope again tomorrow.

11:45.600 --> 11:46.400
You compress it.

11:46.580 --> 11:49.160
Which takes us to the third major section of the monograph.

11:49.520 --> 11:53.160
Right, because once you've hacked your way through the thickest part of the forest,

11:53.160 --> 11:55.060
you don't want to leave it overgrown.

11:55.600 --> 11:56.540
So you save it.

11:57.520 --> 11:59.560
But Flixian is very explicit here.

12:00.080 --> 12:02.320
All representation is compressive.

12:02.480 --> 12:03.240
It has to be.

12:03.520 --> 12:05.720
It is physically and computationally impossible

12:05.720 --> 12:09.260
to record the high-dimensional reality of your entire thought process.

12:09.360 --> 12:10.340
Because it's too messy.

12:10.540 --> 12:12.240
Think about how many false starts,

12:12.640 --> 12:13.660
moments of hesitation,

12:14.180 --> 12:18.480
and subtle contextual feelings you have when solving a hard problem.

12:18.480 --> 12:22.660
You do not have the biological resources to save all of that.

12:23.000 --> 12:25.620
So you project it onto a lower-dimensional manifold.

12:26.160 --> 12:27.820
You throw away all the detritus

12:27.820 --> 12:31.080
and save only the structural skeleton you need for reentry.

12:31.400 --> 12:33.000
You just leave the blazes on the trees.

12:33.120 --> 12:33.600
Exactly.

12:34.000 --> 12:36.600
And this introduces a mathematical proof in the text

12:36.600 --> 12:38.420
called the closure lemma.

12:38.720 --> 12:41.620
Now, my eyes usually glaze over at mathematical proofs, I'll admit.

12:41.720 --> 12:42.200
Oh, same here.

12:42.260 --> 12:44.020
But the implication of this one is massive.

12:44.660 --> 12:48.180
The closure lemma proves that admissibility-preserving compressions

12:48.180 --> 12:49.780
can compose with one another.

12:49.860 --> 12:50.820
Meaning they can stack.

12:51.040 --> 12:51.380
Yes.

12:52.340 --> 12:54.680
Which is the literal mathematical reason

12:54.680 --> 12:56.920
why human knowledge can stack infinitely.

12:57.240 --> 12:59.240
It guarantees that if you compress one thought,

12:59.400 --> 13:02.400
you can safely use it as the starting point for the next thought

13:02.400 --> 13:04.340
without the whole structure collapsing.

13:04.340 --> 13:07.560
Which brings us to the single most fascinating clipboard

13:07.560 --> 13:08.800
in the entire paper.

13:09.440 --> 13:09.960
The denouement.

13:10.200 --> 13:11.700
Oh, the denouement is brilliant.

13:11.840 --> 13:11.980
Right.

13:12.080 --> 13:14.820
So in literature, the denouement is the final resolution of a story,

13:15.000 --> 13:15.920
tying up the loose ends.

13:15.920 --> 13:19.380
But here, a denouement is a compressed traversal

13:19.380 --> 13:20.760
through a high-curvature region

13:20.760 --> 13:23.840
that permanently reduces the local semantic curvature.

13:24.400 --> 13:25.420
It flattens the space.

13:25.540 --> 13:25.780
Yes.

13:26.260 --> 13:27.640
And this blew my mind.

13:27.960 --> 13:29.780
Thesis 5 argues that an essay,

13:29.940 --> 13:31.720
like a written article or a book chapter,

13:31.980 --> 13:33.920
is literally a mathematical function.

13:34.400 --> 13:36.820
Flexion frames it in the language of category theory.

13:37.300 --> 13:39.760
An essay is a morphism in the category trap.

13:39.940 --> 13:41.040
It takes a pressure point,

13:41.040 --> 13:44.240
a chaotic, ambiguous mess of ideas as its input.

13:44.720 --> 13:48.160
And it returns a re-enterable, stable traversal as its output.

13:48.420 --> 13:50.140
It's a curvature-minimizing operator.

13:50.300 --> 13:51.340
Now, I write a lot.

13:51.520 --> 13:54.240
And at first, calling my writing a curvature-minimizing operator

13:54.240 --> 13:55.660
felt, I don't know, a bit reductive.

13:55.740 --> 13:56.420
Like, what about art?

13:56.500 --> 13:57.300
What about creativity?

13:57.640 --> 13:57.880
Right.

13:57.940 --> 13:59.080
It sounds very mechanical.

13:59.280 --> 14:01.080
But then I realized how accurate it is.

14:01.220 --> 14:02.560
Let me try a different analogy on you.

14:02.600 --> 14:03.120
Go for it.

14:03.520 --> 14:04.700
Writing a difficult essay

14:04.700 --> 14:07.500
is like paving a dirt road through the wilderness.

14:08.400 --> 14:09.580
The first time you walk it,

14:09.980 --> 14:11.360
which is the actual thinking phase,

14:12.120 --> 14:13.840
it is incredibly muddy.

14:14.420 --> 14:15.260
It's exhausting.

14:15.780 --> 14:16.980
You get lost.

14:17.400 --> 14:18.520
You have to backtrack.

14:18.620 --> 14:20.560
You are literally cutting down brush.

14:20.940 --> 14:23.400
It is an extremely high-curvature experience.

14:23.500 --> 14:23.880
Exactly.

14:24.280 --> 14:25.820
But the finished essay,

14:26.220 --> 14:27.340
that is the asphalt.

14:27.860 --> 14:29.760
It is a page row that allows you

14:29.760 --> 14:31.820
and any reader 50 years from now

14:31.820 --> 14:35.000
to glide over that exact same intellectual terrain

14:35.000 --> 14:36.500
at 60 miles an hour

14:36.500 --> 14:38.180
without ever getting your boots muddy.

14:38.720 --> 14:41.480
That is precisely the functional utility of the essay.

14:41.620 --> 14:43.960
The author does all the grueling geometric optimization

14:43.960 --> 14:45.320
so the reader doesn't have to.

14:45.640 --> 14:47.640
And when you start stacking these paved roads

14:47.640 --> 14:48.480
on top of each other,

14:48.580 --> 14:51.100
you get what Flixion calls a denouement cascade.

14:51.420 --> 14:52.640
Oh, explain the cascade.

14:52.840 --> 14:53.980
Because this is where we transition

14:53.980 --> 14:55.260
from individual brains

14:55.260 --> 14:57.220
to, like, the history of science.

14:57.220 --> 14:58.460
The denouement cascade

14:58.460 --> 15:00.220
is how deep understanding evolves

15:00.220 --> 15:03.120
in fields like mathematics or physics.

15:04.200 --> 15:05.720
So, centuries ago,

15:05.840 --> 15:07.860
understanding the basic physics of motion

15:07.860 --> 15:09.460
was a high-curvature problem.

15:09.900 --> 15:12.100
It took a genius like Isaac Newton

15:12.100 --> 15:14.380
to stabilize a traversal

15:14.380 --> 15:16.400
through that ambiguity space.

15:16.480 --> 15:18.600
It was a massive intellectual struggle for him.

15:18.600 --> 15:18.840
Right.

15:19.220 --> 15:21.760
And he compressed that massive intellectual effort

15:21.760 --> 15:23.980
into calculus and his laws of motion.

15:24.640 --> 15:26.220
Those formulas are denouement.

15:26.480 --> 15:28.360
They act as callable clipboards.

15:28.800 --> 15:29.060
Okay.

15:29.060 --> 15:30.120
I see where this is going.

15:30.240 --> 15:31.760
So, today, a college sophomore

15:31.760 --> 15:33.460
doesn't have to expend the energy

15:33.460 --> 15:35.240
to invent calculus from scratch.

15:35.400 --> 15:36.760
They just invoke the theorem.

15:37.320 --> 15:39.360
They drive on Newton's paved road

15:39.360 --> 15:41.560
to reach an even higher level of abstraction,

15:41.800 --> 15:42.740
maybe quantum mechanics,

15:43.000 --> 15:45.120
and then they start paving a new road there.

15:45.340 --> 15:47.220
It's a cascade of decreasing curvature

15:47.220 --> 15:49.100
that just continuously pushes

15:49.100 --> 15:50.900
the frontier of human capability forward.

15:50.980 --> 15:51.460
Exactly.

15:51.680 --> 15:51.860
Okay.

15:52.040 --> 15:52.800
This is brilliant,

15:53.000 --> 15:54.200
but it is very theoretical.

15:54.380 --> 15:55.580
I mean, we're talking about topology

15:55.580 --> 15:57.100
and categories and manifolds,

15:57.500 --> 15:59.520
but Flixchan doesn't stay in the clouds.

15:59.720 --> 16:01.340
No, they bring it right down to earth.

16:01.640 --> 16:01.880
Right.

16:01.980 --> 16:02.280
They say,

16:02.440 --> 16:03.500
if this math is truly

16:03.500 --> 16:05.000
the fundamental shape of thought,

16:05.080 --> 16:06.340
we should see it reflected

16:06.340 --> 16:09.200
in the physical and digital tools we build.

16:09.580 --> 16:10.840
Like, when I look at my computer,

16:10.960 --> 16:12.120
is it using these clipboards?

16:12.400 --> 16:13.220
And Flixchan says,

16:13.340 --> 16:14.160
absolutely yes.

16:14.500 --> 16:16.220
It is the ubiquity

16:16.220 --> 16:17.820
of externalized clipboards.

16:17.820 --> 16:20.880
We unconsciously build our digital tools

16:20.880 --> 16:23.200
to mimic the reconstructive traversal

16:23.200 --> 16:24.340
of our own minds.

16:24.500 --> 16:25.760
Let's walk through some of these

16:25.760 --> 16:27.840
because this is where the theory hits reality.

16:28.340 --> 16:29.760
Let's start with Unix pipes

16:29.760 --> 16:31.000
for the programmers listening.

16:31.580 --> 16:31.800
You know,

16:31.820 --> 16:32.820
the little vertical bar

16:32.820 --> 16:34.000
you use in a command line.

16:34.140 --> 16:34.340
Right.

16:34.440 --> 16:35.500
So in a Unix shell,

16:35.700 --> 16:36.960
a pipe takes the output

16:36.960 --> 16:38.480
of one isolated process

16:38.480 --> 16:39.840
and feeds it directly

16:39.840 --> 16:41.040
as the input to the next.

16:41.360 --> 16:42.780
You might list all files,

16:43.100 --> 16:44.400
pipe that into a search tool,

16:44.600 --> 16:46.440
and pipe that into a text file.

16:46.440 --> 16:47.800
And under this framework,

16:48.140 --> 16:50.020
that is sequential clipboard composition.

16:50.160 --> 16:50.580
Exactly.

16:51.220 --> 16:52.400
Process A executes

16:52.400 --> 16:53.880
a compressed traversal

16:53.880 --> 16:55.320
over a messy space of data,

16:55.920 --> 16:56.820
stabilizes it,

16:56.940 --> 16:58.460
and passes that stable result

16:58.460 --> 16:59.880
directly to process B.

17:00.100 --> 17:01.120
What about Git commits?

17:01.580 --> 17:02.720
I use GitHub every day,

17:02.920 --> 17:04.460
and Flixchan's description of a commit

17:04.460 --> 17:05.700
completely changed

17:05.700 --> 17:06.840
how I see my own code.

17:07.080 --> 17:07.680
A Git commit

17:07.680 --> 17:09.240
is the perfect paradigm

17:09.240 --> 17:10.500
of a denouement sequence.

17:11.200 --> 17:11.760
Think about it.

17:12.000 --> 17:13.520
The code base exists

17:13.520 --> 17:14.680
in a state of ambiguity.

17:14.680 --> 17:17.440
A bug is causing the system

17:17.440 --> 17:18.860
to behave unpredictably.

17:19.180 --> 17:20.140
Which is very high curvature.

17:20.400 --> 17:20.560
Right.

17:20.720 --> 17:21.640
Very stressful.

17:21.760 --> 17:21.880
Right.

17:22.220 --> 17:23.340
You, the developer,

17:24.120 --> 17:25.300
navigate that ambiguity,

17:25.660 --> 17:26.940
you isolate the problem,

17:27.060 --> 17:28.180
and you change the code.

17:28.520 --> 17:29.460
Now, the diff,

17:29.780 --> 17:30.880
the actual lines of code

17:30.880 --> 17:31.580
that changed,

17:31.920 --> 17:33.120
shows what was done.

17:33.280 --> 17:33.500
Right.

17:33.580 --> 17:34.660
But the commit message,

17:35.180 --> 17:37.040
that is the human-readable

17:37.040 --> 17:38.260
denouement explaining why.

17:38.540 --> 17:40.200
It permanently resolves

17:40.200 --> 17:40.820
the ambiguity

17:40.820 --> 17:41.960
and freezes it

17:41.960 --> 17:44.160
into an admissibility-preserving state.

17:44.160 --> 17:45.400
So if another developer

17:45.400 --> 17:46.780
gets lost six months later?

17:47.000 --> 17:47.740
They can literally

17:47.740 --> 17:48.680
check out that commit

17:48.680 --> 17:50.120
and re-enter your exact

17:50.120 --> 17:50.980
cognitive state

17:50.980 --> 17:52.220
from that Tuesday afternoon.

17:52.440 --> 17:53.240
That is wild.

17:54.080 --> 17:55.400
And speaking of freezing states,

17:55.640 --> 17:56.240
there's an example

17:56.240 --> 17:58.020
that gamers will instantly recognize.

17:58.440 --> 17:59.160
Save states.

17:59.740 --> 18:00.960
Not just taking a screenshot

18:00.960 --> 18:01.800
of a video game,

18:01.880 --> 18:03.920
but a true emulator save state.

18:04.020 --> 18:05.000
This is a crucial distinction.

18:05.380 --> 18:06.260
A simple screenshot

18:06.260 --> 18:07.780
only captures visual data.

18:07.920 --> 18:09.140
It has no affordance structure.

18:09.380 --> 18:10.140
You can't interact

18:10.140 --> 18:10.840
with a screenshot.

18:11.040 --> 18:11.600
It's just a picture.

18:11.600 --> 18:13.160
But a true save state

18:13.160 --> 18:15.580
is an admissibility-preserving freeze.

18:16.280 --> 18:18.300
It captures the exact variables,

18:18.520 --> 18:19.600
the positions of the enemies,

18:19.800 --> 18:21.020
the momentum of your character,

18:21.400 --> 18:23.000
the exact state of the memory.

18:23.120 --> 18:23.680
So when you load

18:23.680 --> 18:24.460
that save state,

18:24.920 --> 18:26.000
you have all the exact

18:26.000 --> 18:27.500
same navigational possibilities

18:27.500 --> 18:28.840
you had when you pressed save.

18:29.100 --> 18:30.360
It perfectly preserves

18:30.360 --> 18:31.580
the trajectory space.

18:31.860 --> 18:33.420
It is a literal clipboard

18:33.420 --> 18:34.740
of a digital world.

18:34.920 --> 18:35.580
But the example

18:35.580 --> 18:36.360
that hit me the hardest,

18:36.600 --> 18:37.640
the one we teased

18:37.640 --> 18:38.320
at the very beginning

18:38.320 --> 18:39.060
of the deep dive

18:39.060 --> 18:40.320
is browser tabs.

18:41.180 --> 18:43.720
I am a chronic tab hoarder.

18:44.100 --> 18:45.960
I will have four different windows open,

18:46.080 --> 18:47.140
each with 20 tabs,

18:47.560 --> 18:48.240
news articles,

18:48.420 --> 18:49.220
Wikipedia pages,

18:49.380 --> 18:50.120
half-written emails.

18:50.280 --> 18:51.040
It's so common.

18:51.260 --> 18:53.560
And Fliction pathologizes

18:53.560 --> 18:54.740
this behavior perfectly.

18:55.380 --> 18:56.220
They call an array

18:56.220 --> 18:57.120
of browser tabs

18:57.120 --> 18:59.120
an externalized clipboard manifold.

18:59.260 --> 19:00.620
And the reason you hoard them,

19:00.680 --> 19:01.440
the actual reason

19:01.440 --> 19:02.160
it causes you

19:02.160 --> 19:03.160
that mild anxiety

19:03.160 --> 19:03.940
is because of

19:03.940 --> 19:04.640
incomplete

19:04.640 --> 19:05.560
new malformation.

19:05.740 --> 19:06.460
Okay, ouch.

19:06.460 --> 19:07.680
I felt so attacked

19:07.680 --> 19:08.320
by that phrase.

19:08.780 --> 19:09.180
Incomplete

19:09.180 --> 19:10.000
to new malformation.

19:10.360 --> 19:10.780
Walk me through

19:10.780 --> 19:11.800
exactly what is happening

19:11.800 --> 19:12.420
in my brain

19:12.420 --> 19:12.860
when I leave

19:12.860 --> 19:13.940
a Wikipedia tab open

19:13.940 --> 19:14.820
about, I don't know,

19:14.960 --> 19:15.280
the history

19:15.280 --> 19:16.240
of the Byzantine Empire.

19:16.520 --> 19:18.540
So when you open that tab,

19:18.860 --> 19:19.840
you are initiating

19:19.840 --> 19:20.660
a traversal

19:20.660 --> 19:22.200
into an ambiguity space.

19:22.460 --> 19:22.580
Yeah.

19:22.620 --> 19:23.380
You have a question

19:23.380 --> 19:24.620
and you are trying

19:24.620 --> 19:25.460
to find a path

19:25.460 --> 19:26.140
to the answer.

19:26.380 --> 19:27.440
But maybe the article

19:27.440 --> 19:28.740
is incredibly dense.

19:28.900 --> 19:29.740
The semantic curvature

19:29.740 --> 19:30.200
is high.

19:30.300 --> 19:30.680
Exactly.

19:31.180 --> 19:32.000
You get tired

19:32.000 --> 19:33.160
or you get an email

19:33.160 --> 19:33.960
and get distracted.

19:34.260 --> 19:34.940
You haven't resolved

19:34.940 --> 19:35.560
the ambiguity.

19:35.560 --> 19:36.400
You haven't reached

19:36.400 --> 19:37.680
a stable fixed point

19:37.680 --> 19:38.220
where you can say,

19:38.320 --> 19:39.840
ah, I understand this now.

19:39.920 --> 19:40.860
Oh, I don't close the tab.

19:41.180 --> 19:41.980
You leave it open

19:41.980 --> 19:43.460
and that open tab

19:43.460 --> 19:45.020
acts as a persistent,

19:45.560 --> 19:47.060
uncompressed pressure point

19:47.060 --> 19:48.180
in your externalized

19:48.180 --> 19:48.860
working memory.

19:49.240 --> 19:50.400
It's a traversal

19:50.400 --> 19:51.920
that is paused midway.

19:52.140 --> 19:52.820
It is demanding

19:52.820 --> 19:53.340
to be finished.

19:53.440 --> 19:53.740
Yes.

19:54.500 --> 19:55.420
It wants to be finished

19:55.420 --> 19:56.160
but you haven't done

19:56.160 --> 19:57.040
the cognitive work

19:57.040 --> 19:57.760
to compress it

19:57.760 --> 19:58.420
into a denouement.

19:58.620 --> 20:00.140
So my 80 open tabs

20:00.140 --> 20:01.360
are literally

20:01.360 --> 20:03.180
80 mathematical failures

20:03.180 --> 20:04.200
to minimize

20:04.200 --> 20:05.200
cognitive curvature.

20:05.200 --> 20:06.560
Yeah, they are 80 open loops.

20:06.920 --> 20:07.480
No wonder I feel

20:07.480 --> 20:08.360
stressed looking at them.

20:08.440 --> 20:08.900
I haven't paved

20:08.900 --> 20:10.000
any of those dirt roads.

20:10.500 --> 20:11.220
But conversely,

20:11.660 --> 20:12.880
the text looks at tools

20:12.880 --> 20:14.060
designed specifically

20:14.060 --> 20:15.380
for optimal compression.

20:16.100 --> 20:17.300
Things like Cornell notes.

20:17.600 --> 20:18.200
Cornell notes

20:18.200 --> 20:19.180
are a brilliant

20:19.180 --> 20:20.500
physical technology.

20:20.800 --> 20:21.700
You divide the paper,

20:22.420 --> 20:23.540
a Q column on the left,

20:23.820 --> 20:24.780
main notes on the right,

20:24.940 --> 20:26.180
and a summary at the bottom.

20:26.620 --> 20:27.640
Fliction describes this

20:27.640 --> 20:30.240
as a layered projection architecture.

20:30.800 --> 20:31.160
Meaning,

20:31.520 --> 20:32.700
I can access the memory

20:32.700 --> 20:33.540
at different depths

20:33.540 --> 20:34.520
depending on what I need.

20:34.520 --> 20:35.080
Exactly.

20:35.700 --> 20:36.660
The main notes area

20:36.660 --> 20:38.080
is the full traversal.

20:38.460 --> 20:39.240
It has the least amount

20:39.240 --> 20:39.740
of compression.

20:40.000 --> 20:40.820
It's the raw data

20:40.820 --> 20:41.500
of the lecture.

20:41.900 --> 20:42.740
The summary at the bottom

20:42.740 --> 20:43.640
is the denouement,

20:43.840 --> 20:44.780
the highly compressed,

20:45.300 --> 20:46.280
stable conclusion.

20:46.780 --> 20:47.580
And the left column.

20:47.800 --> 20:48.900
The Q column on the left

20:48.900 --> 20:49.880
contains the minimal

20:49.880 --> 20:50.920
retrieval triggers

20:50.920 --> 20:52.020
needed to spark

20:52.020 --> 20:53.140
the aphoric synchrony.

20:53.680 --> 20:54.760
You have built a clipboard

20:54.760 --> 20:55.680
that allows your brain

20:55.680 --> 20:56.980
to re-enter the material

20:56.980 --> 20:58.220
at three different levels

20:58.220 --> 20:59.300
of geometric density

20:59.300 --> 21:00.200
depending on how much

21:00.200 --> 21:00.740
time you have.

21:00.740 --> 21:01.780
And what about flashcards?

21:02.520 --> 21:04.400
Or space repetition apps

21:04.400 --> 21:04.900
like Anki?

21:05.180 --> 21:06.020
I know med students

21:06.020 --> 21:07.460
practically live inside Anki.

21:07.900 --> 21:09.720
Flashcards are what Flixion calls

21:09.720 --> 21:11.540
minimal projection kernels.

21:11.940 --> 21:12.820
They strip away

21:12.820 --> 21:13.880
almost everything,

21:14.320 --> 21:15.240
leaving just the Q

21:15.240 --> 21:16.520
and a minimal resolution.

21:17.300 --> 21:18.180
And the reason

21:18.180 --> 21:19.500
space repetition algorithms

21:19.500 --> 21:20.420
work so perfectly

21:20.420 --> 21:21.200
is because

21:21.200 --> 21:22.780
they interact mathematically

21:22.780 --> 21:24.620
with something Flixion calls

21:24.620 --> 21:26.660
the decay of traversal accessibility.

21:26.660 --> 21:27.220
Right.

21:27.320 --> 21:28.000
Think of the famous

21:28.000 --> 21:29.540
Ebbinghaus forgetting curve.

21:29.940 --> 21:30.060
Right.

21:30.240 --> 21:31.520
The 19th century study

21:31.520 --> 21:33.140
showing that if you learn something,

21:33.560 --> 21:34.780
you forget it exponentially

21:34.780 --> 21:35.580
over time

21:35.580 --> 21:37.520
unless you actively review it.

21:37.700 --> 21:38.420
In classical terms,

21:38.500 --> 21:39.180
we think of forgetting

21:39.180 --> 21:40.020
as the file

21:40.020 --> 21:41.120
just degrading

21:41.120 --> 21:41.880
on the hard drive.

21:42.200 --> 21:42.820
Data rot.

21:43.000 --> 21:44.080
But in Flixion's framework,

21:44.220 --> 21:44.880
forgetting is not

21:44.880 --> 21:45.680
a loss of data.

21:46.380 --> 21:47.520
Forgetting is the natural,

21:47.740 --> 21:48.580
inevitable increase

21:48.580 --> 21:50.020
of accessibility entropy

21:50.020 --> 21:50.720
over time.

21:51.060 --> 21:51.920
Accessibility entropy.

21:51.940 --> 21:52.240
Yeah.

21:52.620 --> 21:54.220
The traversal density decays,

21:54.440 --> 21:55.640
the path through the forest

21:55.640 --> 21:56.840
overgrows with weeds,

21:57.060 --> 21:58.060
the blazes on the trees

21:58.060 --> 21:58.720
fade away.

21:58.960 --> 21:59.940
So space repetition

21:59.940 --> 22:01.280
forces you to re-enter

22:01.280 --> 22:02.120
the traversal

22:02.120 --> 22:03.440
just before the entropy

22:03.440 --> 22:04.160
gets too high.

22:04.360 --> 22:04.840
Exactly.

22:05.900 --> 22:07.160
By walking the path again,

22:07.280 --> 22:08.020
you clear the weeds

22:08.020 --> 22:08.920
and keep the local

22:08.920 --> 22:10.320
traversal density high.

22:10.580 --> 22:12.040
Okay, so if these clipboards

22:12.040 --> 22:13.280
are the primitive substrate

22:13.280 --> 22:14.120
of human memory,

22:14.640 --> 22:15.720
our note-taking systems,

22:15.840 --> 22:16.800
and our coding tools,

22:17.800 --> 22:18.840
how does this framework

22:18.840 --> 22:19.720
explain the elephant

22:19.720 --> 22:20.240
in the room?

22:20.240 --> 22:20.540
Ah.

22:21.140 --> 22:22.320
How does it explain

22:22.320 --> 22:23.240
the absolute explosion

22:23.820 --> 22:25.120
in artificial intelligence,

22:25.120 --> 22:27.440
specifically large language models

22:27.440 --> 22:29.220
like ChatGPT or CLAWD?

22:29.620 --> 22:30.560
This is where the paper

22:30.560 --> 22:31.860
stops being just descriptive

22:31.860 --> 22:33.480
and becomes incredibly predictive.

22:34.180 --> 22:34.860
Under this theory,

22:34.980 --> 22:37.560
an LLM is not a database of facts.

22:37.740 --> 22:39.280
It is a navigational engine.

22:40.100 --> 22:41.220
Flixion argues that

22:41.220 --> 22:42.820
a transformer context window,

22:43.000 --> 22:43.660
the amount of text

22:43.660 --> 22:45.240
the AI can process at one time,

22:45.660 --> 22:47.800
is a bounded cognitive sheaf.

22:48.060 --> 22:49.600
A bounded cognitive sheaf.

22:49.600 --> 22:50.700
Let's unpack the jargon.

22:50.960 --> 22:52.120
What is the AI actually doing

22:52.120 --> 22:53.160
when it reads my prompt

22:53.160 --> 22:54.280
and generates text?

22:54.280 --> 22:55.620
The core of modern AI

22:55.620 --> 22:57.260
is the attention mechanism.

22:57.780 --> 22:58.380
It's the math

22:58.380 --> 22:59.180
that allows the network

22:59.180 --> 22:59.760
to figure out

22:59.760 --> 23:00.700
which words are relevant

23:00.700 --> 23:01.420
to which other words

23:01.420 --> 23:01.980
in a sentence.

23:02.380 --> 23:02.980
Flixion claims

23:02.980 --> 23:03.920
the attention mechanism

23:03.920 --> 23:05.120
is actually a continuous,

23:05.380 --> 23:06.200
differentiable version

23:06.200 --> 23:07.180
of the exact same

23:07.180 --> 23:08.640
euphoric re-entry we use.

23:08.880 --> 23:09.360
Wait, really?

23:09.540 --> 23:10.240
So instead of a human

23:10.240 --> 23:11.260
looking at a journal entry

23:11.260 --> 23:12.040
and synchronizing

23:12.040 --> 23:12.920
with a past thought,

23:13.400 --> 23:14.520
the AI is looking

23:14.520 --> 23:15.680
at the previous tokens,

23:15.880 --> 23:16.780
the previous words

23:16.780 --> 23:17.360
it generated,

23:18.100 --> 23:19.380
and synchronizing with them?

23:19.560 --> 23:20.080
Precisely.

23:20.480 --> 23:21.960
The tokens act as clipboards.

23:21.960 --> 23:23.760
They trigger each other's re-entry

23:23.760 --> 23:25.300
based on admissibility weights.

23:25.820 --> 23:28.060
The AI doesn't know facts.

23:28.620 --> 23:30.080
It is continuously calculating

23:30.080 --> 23:31.120
the most mathematically

23:31.120 --> 23:32.360
coherent path

23:32.360 --> 23:33.660
through the ambiguity space

23:33.660 --> 23:35.320
constrained by the tokens

23:35.320 --> 23:36.220
that came before it.

23:36.300 --> 23:37.280
It is carving a path

23:37.280 --> 23:37.840
through the forest

23:37.840 --> 23:38.960
based on the blazes

23:38.960 --> 23:40.140
you set up in your prompt.

23:40.240 --> 23:41.000
That's exactly it.

23:41.060 --> 23:42.580
But AI hallucinates.

23:43.020 --> 23:44.440
It confidently makes things up.

23:44.560 --> 23:45.900
If it's doing this elegant

23:45.900 --> 23:47.240
mathematical traversal,

23:47.580 --> 23:48.500
why does it tell me

23:48.500 --> 23:49.700
that the first man on the moon

23:49.700 --> 23:51.060
was an 18th century pirate

23:51.060 --> 23:52.380
if I ask it the wrong way?

23:52.780 --> 23:54.180
How does the clipboard framework

23:54.180 --> 23:55.520
explain hallucinations?

23:55.940 --> 23:57.260
Fliction gives hallucination

23:57.260 --> 23:58.380
a very specific

23:58.380 --> 23:59.680
mathematical definition,

24:00.160 --> 24:01.620
admissibility collapse.

24:01.860 --> 24:02.860
Admissibility collapse.

24:02.960 --> 24:03.160
Okay.

24:03.460 --> 24:04.280
To understand this,

24:04.360 --> 24:05.080
we need to introduce

24:05.080 --> 24:06.480
the two opposing forces

24:06.480 --> 24:07.880
that exist in every region

24:07.880 --> 24:09.020
of the semantic manifold.

24:09.680 --> 24:11.420
First, semantic coherence,

24:11.740 --> 24:13.340
represented by the Greek letter phi.

24:13.700 --> 24:16.320
And second, accessibility entropy,

24:16.820 --> 24:18.120
represented by the letter S.

24:18.120 --> 24:20.120
So phi is how coherent

24:20.120 --> 24:22.040
and well-connected the idea is,

24:22.400 --> 24:23.840
and S is the entropy

24:23.840 --> 24:24.720
or the difficulty

24:24.720 --> 24:26.020
of navigating that space.

24:26.080 --> 24:26.260
Right.

24:26.440 --> 24:28.140
Now imagine you prompt the AI

24:28.140 --> 24:29.820
into a highly obscure region

24:29.820 --> 24:31.000
of ambiguity space.

24:31.200 --> 24:32.400
Say you ask it to write

24:32.400 --> 24:34.260
a detailed comparative analysis

24:34.260 --> 24:35.980
of a completely obscure

24:35.980 --> 24:37.260
14th century poet

24:37.260 --> 24:39.600
and the aerodynamics

24:39.600 --> 24:40.420
of a helicopter.

24:40.740 --> 24:41.920
A topic it definitely

24:41.920 --> 24:42.600
does not have

24:42.600 --> 24:43.680
dense training data for.

24:43.720 --> 24:44.320
Exactly.

24:44.600 --> 24:45.540
Because the training data

24:45.540 --> 24:46.280
is sparse there,

24:46.280 --> 24:47.540
the traversal pathways

24:47.540 --> 24:48.880
are almost non-existent.

24:49.080 --> 24:50.700
The accessibility entropy S

24:50.700 --> 24:51.500
skyrockets.

24:51.840 --> 24:52.900
And here is the breaking point.

24:53.280 --> 24:54.100
If the entropy S

24:54.100 --> 24:56.120
exceeds the semantic coherence phi,

24:56.600 --> 24:58.200
the admissibility kernel collapses.

24:58.520 --> 24:59.700
The region simply cannot

24:59.700 --> 25:01.340
sustain a stable traversal.

25:01.440 --> 25:02.500
There is no path

25:02.500 --> 25:03.100
through the forest.

25:03.440 --> 25:04.880
But the LLM is a machine.

25:05.100 --> 25:06.300
It can't just throw its hands up

25:06.300 --> 25:07.240
and say I'm lost

25:07.240 --> 25:08.920
unless it's explicitly programmed

25:08.920 --> 25:09.560
to do that.

25:09.780 --> 25:10.440
It has to output

25:10.440 --> 25:11.140
the next token.

25:11.140 --> 25:11.740
Yes.

25:12.300 --> 25:13.900
It is forced to keep walking.

25:14.600 --> 25:16.300
So it outputs a sequence

25:16.300 --> 25:17.240
of words that look

25:17.240 --> 25:18.760
syntactically valid

25:18.760 --> 25:20.280
based on local probabilities,

25:20.600 --> 25:22.400
but it is pure confabulation.

25:22.660 --> 25:23.900
It is no longer grounded

25:23.900 --> 25:26.240
in a stable global traversal.

25:26.400 --> 25:27.400
It has lost the blazes

25:27.400 --> 25:28.080
on the trees.

25:28.500 --> 25:29.440
The entropy has overwhelmed

25:29.440 --> 25:30.160
the coherence,

25:30.280 --> 25:31.160
but it keeps generating

25:31.160 --> 25:31.980
tokens anyway.

25:32.180 --> 25:33.720
That is a hallucination.

25:33.920 --> 25:35.180
That perfectly describes

25:35.180 --> 25:36.340
the confident nonsense

25:36.340 --> 25:37.420
an AI can spit out.

25:37.640 --> 25:39.120
It's just syntactically

25:39.120 --> 25:39.980
correct wandering.

25:41.260 --> 25:42.500
So under this framework,

25:42.720 --> 25:43.880
what actually is

25:43.880 --> 25:44.720
prompt engineering?

25:44.920 --> 25:46.620
When I type a highly specific

25:46.620 --> 25:48.520
multi-paragraph prompt

25:48.520 --> 25:49.880
giving the AI a persona,

25:50.240 --> 25:51.200
rules, and context,

25:51.500 --> 25:53.500
what am I mathematically doing?

25:53.880 --> 25:54.740
Prompt engineering

25:54.740 --> 25:56.340
is injecting boundary conditions

25:56.340 --> 25:57.560
into a semantic flow.

25:58.280 --> 25:59.440
You are artificially setting

25:59.440 --> 26:00.780
the values of phi and S

26:00.780 --> 26:01.300
at the borders

26:01.300 --> 26:02.380
of the ambiguity space.

26:02.400 --> 26:02.780
I love.

26:03.040 --> 26:04.580
By providing strict context,

26:04.920 --> 26:06.360
you are constraining the AI

26:06.360 --> 26:08.240
so that the only stable attractors

26:08.240 --> 26:08.860
it can fall into

26:08.860 --> 26:09.760
who are the ones that align

26:09.760 --> 26:10.860
with your desired output.

26:11.260 --> 26:12.580
You are literally building fences

26:12.580 --> 26:13.160
in the forest

26:13.160 --> 26:14.220
to force the AI down

26:14.220 --> 26:15.020
a specific path

26:15.020 --> 26:16.440
and prevent it from wandering

26:16.440 --> 26:17.680
into high entropy zones.

26:17.860 --> 26:18.800
Hold on, let me stop you there

26:18.800 --> 26:19.340
because this is where

26:19.340 --> 26:20.440
I get a little uneasy.

26:21.180 --> 26:22.240
If the AI is using

26:22.240 --> 26:24.340
the exact same attention mechanism

26:24.340 --> 26:25.640
and clipboard re-entry

26:25.640 --> 26:27.300
that my human cognition uses,

26:27.780 --> 26:29.060
what is the actual difference

26:29.060 --> 26:30.800
between my mind and the LLM?

26:31.200 --> 26:32.880
Are you saying my consciousness

26:32.880 --> 26:34.560
is just predicting the next token?

26:35.000 --> 26:36.420
No, and Flixian draws

26:36.420 --> 26:37.900
a very hard line here.

26:37.900 --> 26:38.740
The key difference

26:38.740 --> 26:39.300
is in the phrase

26:39.300 --> 26:40.040
I used earlier,

26:40.520 --> 26:42.140
a bounded cognitive sheaf.

26:42.800 --> 26:43.820
The word bounded

26:43.820 --> 26:44.980
is everything.

26:45.180 --> 26:46.040
Explain the boundary.

26:46.120 --> 26:47.540
LLMs have a hard

26:47.540 --> 26:48.900
architectural limit.

26:49.540 --> 26:51.160
Their context window parameter,

26:51.320 --> 26:52.580
often denoted as N,

26:52.800 --> 26:54.160
whether it's 8,000 tokens

26:54.160 --> 26:55.420
or a million tokens,

26:55.700 --> 26:57.700
it is a finite closed system.

26:57.980 --> 26:58.820
They can only hold

26:58.820 --> 27:00.180
that exact number of clipboards

27:00.180 --> 27:01.020
in their local sheaf

27:01.020 --> 27:01.680
at one time.

27:01.760 --> 27:02.700
And once it passes

27:02.700 --> 27:03.360
that boundary?

27:03.360 --> 27:04.860
The earliest tokens vanish.

27:05.440 --> 27:06.900
The system forgets them completely.

27:07.560 --> 27:08.720
Human cognitive sheaves,

27:08.800 --> 27:09.420
on the other hand,

27:09.460 --> 27:10.360
are theoretically unbounded.

27:11.100 --> 27:11.800
But my working memory

27:11.800 --> 27:12.340
is caribble.

27:12.420 --> 27:13.300
I can barely remember

27:13.300 --> 27:13.820
a phone number.

27:13.860 --> 27:15.140
Your biological working memory

27:15.140 --> 27:15.940
is limited, yes,

27:16.060 --> 27:17.580
but your global cognitive sheaf

27:17.580 --> 27:18.360
is unbounded

27:18.360 --> 27:19.760
because you can recursively

27:19.760 --> 27:21.200
fold infinite external

27:21.200 --> 27:22.440
contexts into it.

27:22.620 --> 27:23.520
Right, through my journals,

27:23.640 --> 27:24.280
the books I read,

27:24.360 --> 27:25.400
the conversations I have.

27:25.440 --> 27:25.780
Exactly.

27:25.980 --> 27:27.380
You continuously link

27:27.380 --> 27:28.500
local clipboards

27:28.500 --> 27:29.360
into a massive,

27:29.600 --> 27:30.520
lifelong architecture.

27:30.520 --> 27:32.260
You can build a deeply

27:32.260 --> 27:33.720
interconnected worldview

27:33.720 --> 27:34.740
over decades.

27:35.300 --> 27:36.440
The AI is trapped

27:36.440 --> 27:37.180
within a hard

27:37.180 --> 27:38.340
mathematical boundary.

27:39.100 --> 27:39.860
Every time you open

27:39.860 --> 27:40.640
a new chat,

27:40.800 --> 27:41.820
it starts from zero.

27:42.180 --> 27:42.960
It has no history.

27:43.160 --> 27:44.860
It cannot organically build

27:44.860 --> 27:45.680
an internal,

27:45.940 --> 27:46.960
evolving architecture

27:46.960 --> 27:48.120
of its own states.

27:48.340 --> 27:49.840
It is a phenomenal engine

27:49.840 --> 27:51.120
for local traversals,

27:51.280 --> 27:53.200
but it has no global continuity.

27:53.560 --> 27:54.680
Okay, that makes me feel

27:54.680 --> 27:55.660
slightly less

27:55.660 --> 27:57.240
existentially dread-filled.

27:57.700 --> 27:58.400
But this brings up

27:58.400 --> 27:59.120
a great question.

27:59.120 --> 28:01.320
If my mind is an unbounded

28:01.320 --> 28:02.580
collection of local thoughts,

28:03.100 --> 28:04.300
how do my individual

28:04.300 --> 28:05.140
messy thoughts

28:05.140 --> 28:06.440
actually link together

28:06.440 --> 28:08.120
to form a coherent worldview?

28:08.680 --> 28:09.100
This takes us

28:09.100 --> 28:10.120
into Chapter 12,

28:10.320 --> 28:11.180
The Physics of Meaning.

28:11.520 --> 28:12.500
To explain how beliefs

28:12.500 --> 28:13.120
link together,

28:13.260 --> 28:13.980
Flitchin uses

28:13.980 --> 28:15.420
sheaf-theoretic coherence.

28:15.920 --> 28:16.980
A sheaf is a real,

28:17.120 --> 28:18.440
incredibly complex tool

28:18.440 --> 28:19.120
and typology

28:19.120 --> 28:20.000
used to track

28:20.000 --> 28:20.880
how local data

28:20.880 --> 28:21.680
can be systematically

28:21.680 --> 28:22.480
glued together

28:22.480 --> 28:23.680
to form global data.

28:23.860 --> 28:25.460
So how do my local opinions

28:25.460 --> 28:27.260
on, say, economic policy

28:27.260 --> 28:27.840
glue together

28:27.840 --> 28:28.780
with my local opinions

28:28.780 --> 28:30.220
on ethics or family?

28:30.360 --> 28:30.840
Exactly.

28:31.460 --> 28:32.520
A cognitive sheaf

28:32.520 --> 28:33.280
is the structure

28:33.280 --> 28:34.680
of how your local clipboards

28:34.680 --> 28:36.800
cohere into a unified self.

28:37.440 --> 28:38.520
The text points

28:38.520 --> 28:39.540
to Nicholas Luhmann's

28:39.540 --> 28:41.160
famous Zettelkasten method

28:41.160 --> 28:41.920
as the ultimate

28:41.920 --> 28:42.860
physical manifestation

28:42.860 --> 28:43.560
of this.

28:43.720 --> 28:45.780
Ah, the German sociologist

28:45.780 --> 28:46.740
who wrote thousands

28:46.740 --> 28:48.180
of notes on index cards

28:48.180 --> 28:49.200
and linked them all together

28:49.200 --> 28:50.520
with alphanumeric codes.

28:50.720 --> 28:51.000
Yes.

28:51.560 --> 28:52.300
His slipbox

28:52.300 --> 28:53.240
was a physical,

28:53.620 --> 28:54.580
cognitive sheaf.

28:55.060 --> 28:56.040
Because he forced

28:56.040 --> 28:57.860
every single local note

28:57.860 --> 28:58.580
to interlink

28:58.580 --> 28:59.140
and negotiate

28:59.140 --> 29:00.040
with the other notes,

29:00.380 --> 29:01.160
the system began

29:01.160 --> 29:02.480
to generate surprising

29:02.480 --> 29:03.480
global connections

29:03.480 --> 29:04.340
that he hadn't

29:04.340 --> 29:05.280
consciously planned.

29:05.860 --> 29:06.640
Local sections

29:06.640 --> 29:08.400
cohered into a global section.

29:08.400 --> 29:09.120
But let's be honest

29:09.120 --> 29:10.100
about human nature here.

29:10.180 --> 29:11.000
My thoughts do not

29:11.000 --> 29:12.080
always cohere perfectly.

29:12.600 --> 29:13.240
Sometimes I hold

29:13.240 --> 29:13.920
two beliefs

29:13.920 --> 29:15.260
that completely logically

29:15.260 --> 29:16.200
contradict each other.

29:16.400 --> 29:17.160
And mathematically,

29:17.420 --> 29:18.440
psychology calls that

29:18.440 --> 29:19.620
cognitive dissonance.

29:19.620 --> 29:20.800
But fliction gives it

29:20.800 --> 29:22.880
a precise topological definition.

29:23.520 --> 29:25.660
An H1 cohomological obstruction.

29:26.020 --> 29:28.140
An H1 cohomological obstruction.

29:28.300 --> 29:28.840
Okay, you have to

29:28.840 --> 29:29.800
visualize that for me

29:29.800 --> 29:30.180
because that is

29:30.180 --> 29:31.300
pure math jargon.

29:31.560 --> 29:32.260
What does an obstruction

29:32.260 --> 29:33.120
actually look like

29:33.120 --> 29:33.660
in my brain?

29:33.920 --> 29:34.720
Imagine you have

29:34.720 --> 29:36.220
an atlas made of two maps.

29:36.920 --> 29:38.060
Map A shows the roads

29:38.060 --> 29:38.660
in your city

29:38.660 --> 29:39.600
and Map B

29:39.600 --> 29:40.780
shows the subway lines.

29:41.540 --> 29:41.980
Individually,

29:42.120 --> 29:42.540
they are both

29:42.540 --> 29:44.300
perfectly stable clipboards.

29:44.440 --> 29:45.040
They make sense

29:45.040 --> 29:45.540
on their own.

29:45.640 --> 29:46.420
Okay, I'm with you.

29:46.540 --> 29:47.220
But when you try

29:47.220 --> 29:48.300
to lay them over each other,

29:48.420 --> 29:49.000
when you try to

29:49.000 --> 29:49.600
glue them together

29:49.600 --> 29:50.400
where they overlap,

29:50.840 --> 29:51.520
the subway stops

29:51.520 --> 29:52.080
don't line up

29:52.080 --> 29:52.880
with the street corners.

29:53.080 --> 29:53.740
They clash.

29:54.320 --> 29:55.360
That gap,

29:55.660 --> 29:57.080
that failure to globalize

29:57.080 --> 29:58.900
two stable local states,

29:59.120 --> 30:00.800
is an H1 obstruction.

30:01.060 --> 30:01.940
And it creates friction.

30:02.160 --> 30:02.900
It feels uncomfortable.

30:03.100 --> 30:03.900
Very uncomfortable.

30:04.320 --> 30:05.300
And it gets worse.

30:05.980 --> 30:08.300
Deep foundational paradoxes,

30:08.440 --> 30:09.840
like logical antinomies

30:09.840 --> 30:10.940
that break your brain

30:10.940 --> 30:11.660
no matter how

30:11.660 --> 30:12.560
you try to resolve them,

30:12.820 --> 30:13.500
are classified

30:13.500 --> 30:15.020
as H2 obstructions.

30:15.660 --> 30:16.780
It means the topology

30:16.780 --> 30:17.800
itself is twisted,

30:18.020 --> 30:18.980
like a Mubius strip.

30:18.980 --> 30:19.780
You can't ever

30:19.780 --> 30:20.480
flatten it out.

30:21.080 --> 30:21.860
So when I experience

30:21.860 --> 30:22.880
cognitive dissonance,

30:22.940 --> 30:24.040
when my maps don't line up,

30:24.100 --> 30:24.720
how do the framework

30:24.720 --> 30:25.560
say I fix it?

30:26.380 --> 30:26.820
Topologically,

30:27.080 --> 30:28.300
there are only three ways

30:28.300 --> 30:29.320
to resolve the clash.

30:29.680 --> 30:30.060
First,

30:30.480 --> 30:31.040
belief revision.

30:31.640 --> 30:32.420
You modify one

30:32.420 --> 30:33.400
of the local clipboards.

30:33.600 --> 30:34.480
You erase a street

30:34.480 --> 30:35.020
on map A

30:35.020 --> 30:36.080
until it fits map B.

30:36.420 --> 30:37.140
You change your mind.

30:37.260 --> 30:38.420
Which requires admitting

30:38.420 --> 30:39.140
you were wrong,

30:39.240 --> 30:40.020
which is hard.

30:40.280 --> 30:41.120
What's the second way?

30:41.360 --> 30:42.280
A paradigm shift.

30:42.780 --> 30:43.520
You modify

30:43.520 --> 30:45.080
the entire base space.

30:45.080 --> 30:46.200
You throw out

30:46.200 --> 30:47.080
the old rules

30:47.080 --> 30:48.340
of geometry entirely

30:48.340 --> 30:49.020
and adopt

30:49.020 --> 30:49.860
a new framework

30:49.860 --> 30:51.260
where the two maps

30:51.260 --> 30:52.680
suddenly make sense together.

30:52.920 --> 30:53.620
Think of shifting

30:53.620 --> 30:54.840
from Newtonian physics

30:54.840 --> 30:56.420
to Einstein's relativity.

30:56.700 --> 30:57.100
Exactly.

30:57.460 --> 30:58.420
It is incredibly

30:58.420 --> 30:59.560
energetically expensive.

31:00.080 --> 31:01.320
It requires tearing up

31:01.320 --> 31:01.960
the foundation

31:01.960 --> 31:02.840
of your worldview.

31:03.400 --> 31:04.000
Which explains

31:04.000 --> 31:04.860
why people fight

31:04.860 --> 31:05.580
paradigm shifts

31:05.580 --> 31:06.280
so fiercely.

31:06.920 --> 31:07.960
And the third option,

31:08.620 --> 31:09.040
I'm going to guess

31:09.040 --> 31:10.080
it's the one most of us

31:10.080 --> 31:11.120
use every single day.

31:11.920 --> 31:12.520
Compartmentalization.

31:12.520 --> 31:13.560
You just accept

31:13.560 --> 31:14.780
the partial coherence.

31:15.120 --> 31:15.640
You acknowledge

31:15.640 --> 31:16.760
the maps don't line up

31:16.760 --> 31:17.440
but you refuse

31:17.440 --> 31:18.220
to let them overlap.

31:18.580 --> 31:19.580
You look at map A

31:19.580 --> 31:20.460
on Tuesdays

31:20.460 --> 31:21.120
and map B

31:21.120 --> 31:21.800
on Thursdays.

31:21.840 --> 31:22.420
You sacrifice

31:22.420 --> 31:23.600
global navigability

31:23.600 --> 31:24.860
to stay locally sane.

31:25.120 --> 31:25.740
You just ignore

31:25.740 --> 31:26.360
the clash.

31:26.700 --> 31:26.920
Wow.

31:27.520 --> 31:28.620
That is brutally accurate.

31:29.340 --> 31:30.020
To manage all

31:30.020 --> 31:31.080
of this dynamic shifting,

31:31.300 --> 31:32.080
Fliction introduces

31:32.080 --> 31:32.780
an equation.

31:33.340 --> 31:34.940
The RSVP field triple.

31:35.380 --> 31:36.040
It consists of

31:36.040 --> 31:37.680
phi, V, and S.

31:37.900 --> 31:38.720
This is what Fliction

31:38.720 --> 31:39.580
calls the physics

31:39.580 --> 31:40.120
of meaning.

31:40.660 --> 31:41.520
We already discussed

31:41.520 --> 31:42.220
phi, which is

31:42.220 --> 31:42.780
coherence,

31:42.960 --> 31:43.360
and S,

31:43.480 --> 31:44.380
accessibility entropy.

31:44.880 --> 31:45.880
But the missing piece

31:45.880 --> 31:47.080
is V, which stands

31:47.080 --> 31:47.820
for velocity.

31:47.980 --> 31:48.380
We didn't talk

31:48.380 --> 31:49.260
about velocity yet.

31:49.300 --> 31:49.780
How does a thought

31:49.780 --> 31:50.380
have a speed?

31:50.900 --> 31:51.940
Velocity, V,

31:52.060 --> 31:52.780
is the speed

31:52.780 --> 31:53.360
and efficiency

31:53.360 --> 31:54.140
with which you can

31:54.140 --> 31:55.320
execute a traversal.

31:55.980 --> 31:57.240
When you are a novice

31:57.240 --> 31:58.040
at playing the piano,

31:58.280 --> 32:00.000
your V is extremely low.

32:00.520 --> 32:01.120
You have to consciously

32:01.120 --> 32:01.940
think about every

32:01.940 --> 32:03.140
single finger placement.

32:03.300 --> 32:04.120
The entropy S

32:04.120 --> 32:05.000
is very high.

32:05.220 --> 32:05.480
Right.

32:05.860 --> 32:07.040
But as you practice,

32:07.300 --> 32:08.140
as you stabilize

32:08.140 --> 32:09.180
those motor cognitive

32:09.180 --> 32:09.840
clipboards,

32:10.240 --> 32:11.480
your density increases.

32:11.480 --> 32:12.780
Your entropy drops

32:12.780 --> 32:13.680
and your velocity

32:13.680 --> 32:14.460
skyrockets.

32:15.080 --> 32:15.580
You can play

32:15.580 --> 32:16.580
a complex sonata

32:16.580 --> 32:17.580
without consciously

32:17.580 --> 32:18.180
thinking about

32:18.180 --> 32:19.240
the individual notes.

32:19.780 --> 32:20.460
The traversal

32:20.460 --> 32:21.480
becomes automatic.

32:21.840 --> 32:22.280
So expertise

32:22.280 --> 32:22.900
isn't just

32:22.900 --> 32:23.880
knowing more things.

32:24.120 --> 32:25.020
It is physically

32:25.020 --> 32:25.820
traversing the

32:25.820 --> 32:26.860
ambiguity space

32:26.860 --> 32:28.400
at a higher velocity

32:28.400 --> 32:29.320
because you've

32:29.320 --> 32:30.020
cleared the paths.

32:30.240 --> 32:30.780
Precisely.

32:31.100 --> 32:32.220
And this trio phi,

32:32.560 --> 32:33.520
V, and S

32:33.520 --> 32:34.240
are governed by

32:34.240 --> 32:35.080
the xylomorphic

32:35.080 --> 32:36.000
stability condition,

32:36.320 --> 32:37.040
the master rule

32:37.040 --> 32:37.600
of cognition.

32:38.300 --> 32:38.740
Phi must be

32:38.740 --> 32:39.540
greater than S.

32:39.540 --> 32:40.640
coherence must

32:40.640 --> 32:41.520
exceed entropy

32:41.520 --> 32:42.300
for a thought

32:42.300 --> 32:43.040
to stabilize

32:43.040 --> 32:43.960
into a clipboard.

32:44.460 --> 32:44.940
I want to dwell

32:44.940 --> 32:45.540
on accessibility

32:45.540 --> 32:46.700
entropy for a second

32:46.700 --> 32:47.740
because there is

32:47.740 --> 32:48.480
an idea in this

32:48.480 --> 32:49.220
section that I found

32:49.220 --> 32:50.300
profoundly poetic,

32:50.540 --> 32:51.460
semantic redshift.

32:51.640 --> 32:52.160
Oh, this is a

32:52.160 --> 32:52.940
beautiful concept.

32:53.260 --> 32:53.460
Right.

32:53.820 --> 32:54.660
In astrophysics,

32:55.000 --> 32:55.840
redshift is how

32:55.840 --> 32:56.840
light from distant

32:56.840 --> 32:57.920
galaxies stretches

32:57.920 --> 32:58.740
out and shifts

32:58.740 --> 32:59.360
to the red end

32:59.360 --> 32:59.920
of the spectrum

32:59.920 --> 33:01.080
because the universe

33:01.080 --> 33:01.740
is expanding.

33:02.460 --> 33:03.320
The farther away

33:03.320 --> 33:04.100
a galaxy is,

33:04.180 --> 33:05.020
the harder it is

33:05.020 --> 33:05.700
to detect.

33:06.420 --> 33:06.980
How does

33:06.980 --> 33:07.660
Fleekshin apply

33:07.660 --> 33:08.460
astrophysics

33:08.460 --> 33:09.320
to human meaning?

33:09.840 --> 33:10.760
Semantic redshift

33:10.760 --> 33:11.640
explains why

33:11.640 --> 33:13.240
very old ideas

33:13.240 --> 33:14.280
or concepts

33:14.280 --> 33:14.860
from highly

33:14.860 --> 33:15.440
specialized

33:15.440 --> 33:16.420
academic disciplines

33:16.420 --> 33:18.680
are so agonizingly

33:18.680 --> 33:19.760
hard to understand

33:19.760 --> 33:20.500
for an outsider.

33:21.100 --> 33:21.800
We often assume

33:21.800 --> 33:22.280
it's because

33:22.280 --> 33:23.500
the ideas themselves

33:23.500 --> 33:24.580
are intrinsically

33:24.580 --> 33:25.600
more complicated

33:25.600 --> 33:26.960
